# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 135's class, lane 135b: the other half of
# `fixedbugs-135-a-dropped-value-of-a-place-s-own-type`. Where a line is kept
# for what a call does and its value is the call's by-product, of no place's
# own type, not a `bool`, not in a `test` and not ending a function that
# returns a value, the discard is the line's one reading and `_ = ` stays
# certain: `check --apply` writes it, in a branch, in a loop, after a call
# whose `@` already wrote its argument back, and in an arm as the block the
# spec names, since `_ = ` on the arm's own line is `declaration_in_arm`.

function compute(n: i64) -> i64
    print(n)
    return n * 2

function note(n: i64) -> str
    print(n)
    return n.to_str()

function step(@xs: [i64]) -> [i64]
    xs @ xs.push(1)
    return xs

function main()
    compute(5)  #~ discarded_value
    xs: [i64] @= []
    step(@xs)  #~ discarded_value
    c = true

    if c
        compute(1)  #~ discarded_value
    else
        compute(2)  #~ discarded_value
    i: i64 @= 0

    while i < 2
        i @ i + 1
        note(i)  #~ discarded_value

    match xs.len()
        0 => compute(0)  #~ discarded_value
        _ => print(xs.len())

    print(xs.len())

# Corrected 2026-10-07, defect 188: the header's last clause reasons from the
# arm rule panel 185's R3 replaced on 2026-10-02. Since R3 spec § 8 reads *An
# arm's body is an indented block, or inline one statement, where a
# declaration or an `=` may name only `_`* and *An arm that does nothing holds
# `_ = 0`*, so `_ = ` on the arm's own line is legal and not
# `declaration_in_arm`, and the spec names no block. `check --apply` still
# writes the arm's discard as a block under the arm, one of the two forms the
# rule allows, and this case pins that it does.
